PUBLIC RESPONSE: F. None of the above

The Book of the Duchess is written entirely in rhyming octosyllabic couplets. Inspection of the text shows that each listed word appears at the end of a line and participates in at least one rhyme pair:
- wente rhymes with hente, mente, etc. (e.g., 44–45, 115–116);
- here rhymes with there, were, etc. (e.g., 78–79, 231–232);
- fool rhymes with hool (e.g., 531–532);
- hool rhymes with fool (same passage) and also with wool, etc.;
- countour rhymes with tour, hour, etc. (e.g., 654–655, 686–687).

Because Chaucer does make rhymes with every word in the list, the correct choice is F.

(The question’s phrasing is slightly ambiguous, but the only reading consistent with the poem’s actual rhyme practice is that no listed word is omitted from rhyming use.)

